//
// Pippin.xcconfig — the three values that differ between the open-source
// project and the app that ships.
//
// WHY THEY ARE BUILD SETTINGS AND NOT SOURCE. Each is something the repository
// must NOT know: the product's App Store name, a contact address that would be
// scraped the day it was pushed, and the Apple Developer team the build signs
// against. Kept in source, the only way to have them locally is an edit that is
// never committed — a permanently dirty working tree, one `git commit -a` away
// from being public. Kept here, the tracked file carries harmless defaults and
// the real values live in a git-ignored file beside it.
//
// THE DEFAULTS ARE THE OPEN-SOURCE ANSWER, and they are a WORKING answer, not
// a placeholder: a clone with no local file builds an app called Pippin whose
// Report a Problem sheet says no address is set and offers the share button
// instead — which is exactly the state `ReportMail` was written to handle.
//
//   PP_DISPLAY_NAME    the name on the home screen, in the share sheet, in
//                      the location permission alert and in every sentence
//                      `AppName.display` puts it in.
//   PP_REPORT_ADDRESS  where Report a Problem mails to. An iCloud Hide My
//                      Email alias — see `ReportMail` in Sharing.swift.
//   PP_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM  the ten-character Apple Developer team id every
//                      target signs against.
//
// The team id is the one of the three that is not secret — it is readable in
// any signed app — but it identifies a personal Apple account and is useless
// to anyone else, so it belongs here with the other two. Empty is the handled
// state: an unsigned build of the C++ and the simulator app needs no team, and
// Xcode's Signing & Capabilities pane or `DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=` on the
// `xcodebuild` line is the other way to supply one.

PP_DISPLAY_NAME = Pippin
PP_REPORT_ADDRESS =
PP_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// THE LOCAL OVERRIDE, and the `?` is the whole point: `#include?` is the
// OPTIONAL spelling, so a checkout without the file builds silently rather
// than warning "unable to open base configuration reference file" at everyone
// who clones this. It is LAST so that what it sets wins.
//
// Write it with `local/set-branding.sh`; `local/` is git-ignored, so neither
// the script nor the values it stores can be committed.
#include? "local/Local.xcconfig"
